  "steps": [
    {
      "text": "Prep ingredients: Peel the pumpkin, then cut into 2cm chunks. Cut the capsicums into 2cm chunks. Trim the beans, then cut into thirds on an angle. Crush or finely chop 2 garlic cloves.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/233509/photos/large/prep_ingredients-b4baddb7367cd210a890ee25e7dd85f7.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Cook quinoa: Put the quinoa in a sieve, then rinse well and drain (see Kitchen 101). Put the quinoa and apricots in a small saucepan with 250ml (1 cup) water, season with salt, cover and bring to a simmer. Reduce the heat to low and cook, covered, for 12-15 mins until the water is absorbed and the quinoa is tender. Turn off the heat and stand, covered, for 5 mins. Stir in 2 tsp olive oil.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/233510/photos/large/cook_quinoa-b15206f7a07e3ec0fd1d1c8e5f561ed2.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Start tagine: Meanwhile, heat 1 tbs olive oil in a medium deep frypan over medium heat. Cook the pumpkin, capsicum, garlic and 1 tbs Moroccan seasoning, stirring, for 1-2 mins until fragrant. Season with salt and pepper.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/233511/photos/large/start_tagine-3d4e29c4f6d4941c87bdfb1ea9541180.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Simmer tagine: Add the tomatoes, 1 tsp honey and 125ml (½ cup) water to the pumpkin mixture and bring to the boil. Reduce the heat to low and cook, covered, for 6-8 mins until the pumpkin is tender.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/233512/photos/large/simmer_tagine-99412a3bcc13e449f5b46818ef891b69.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Serve up: Add the beans and 1 tsp tomato sauce to the pan and cook for 2-3 mins until the beans are tender. Taste, then season with salt and pepper. Divide the apricot quinoa and veggie tagine among bowls and enjoy!",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/233513/photos/large/serve_up-6e55d8cdd9d0908969c34cc78d97d058.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Kitchen 101: It’s important to rinse the quinoa well to remove the natural coating called saponin, which can make it taste bitter or soapy.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/233514/photos/large/kitchen_101-6431fb095f562ba962caa5176c24db44.jpeg"
    }
  ]
}
